    {
      "id": "ledger-notes",
      "kind": {
        "text": "WHY THIS FACT EXISTS. F:int-characterization proved the properties that separate Z from Q and from lexicographic Z[x] HOLD of `Int`, and said in its own notes that it was not proved they DETERMINE it -- only the uniqueness half of the universal property was checked. This closes the gap by constructing the map out of an arbitrary target and proving it a bijection. FRAMING MATTERS: the theorem is about Z-structures (point + automorphism), NOT about discretely ordered rings; `every discretely ordered ring generated by 1 is isomorphic to Int` would need the order axioms as hypotheses and a derivation of the automorphism from them. What is proved is that the order properties hold of Int (F:int-characterization) and that the Z-structure properties determine it (here). WHAT IS NOT PROVED: no inverse FUNCTION is extracted -- surjectivity is a Prop-level existential, exactly as in F:nat-peano-categoricity, because the generation hypothesis is Prop-valued. `iso` gives the constructed two-sided inverse but HYPOTHESISES the back-map. LOAD-BEARING: thirteen new injected `Weakening` variants (22 in the package) replace one hypothesis with `True` and the kernel must refuse each ONE AT THE DECLARATION IT WAS AIMED AT, with the declaration immediately before it in build order still admitted. Both guards were mutation-probed on 2026-08-18: making one injection inert killed exactly one test, and aiming one defect at the wrong declaration made both the test and the example fail.",
        "type": "prose"
      },
      "provenance": {
        "command": "python3 render/producers-py/facts_to_docir.py",
        "epoch": {
          "commit": "733126c0f351f0931f7eaf0b4f5f9f569f3aef44",
